# tools/s/shape_match_witness.rish -- the DISTANCE between two shapes' HU FINGERPRINTS, on metal (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- image/hu_moments.rye reads a component's SEVEN HU INVARIANT MOMENTS, the fingerprint that
# stays (near) constant when a mark is turned, moved, or resized. A fingerprint on its own answers nothing; the fact a surface
# asks is COMPARISON: is this mark the SAME SHAPE as that one? This rung reads that -- one scalar DISTANCE over two Hu sets by
# signed log (the standard shape-comparison transform), small when two marks are the same form and large when they differ. That
# is the move a text reader, an icon matcher, and a part recognizer are built on: not what a shape's fingerprint IS, but how FAR
# one fingerprint sits from another. Compose not braid: this rung owns only the metric; hu_moments owns the invariants, label the
# LabelMap. No pixel is read, no image written, no codec touched -- a pure function over two seven-number fingerprints.
#
# The crux this witness proves on metal: a chiral right triangle turned 90 degrees, mirrored, and scaled up 4x each reads a TINY
# distance to the base triangle, while a genuinely different shape (a symmetric plus) reads a LARGE one -- one number tells "same
# form" from "different form" where no single Hu value can. The reflection-invariant shape_distance over the six invariants i1..i6
# reads a mark and its left-to-right MIRROR as float-exact the same form, while shape_distance_oriented over all seven SEES the
# mirror as different (a real nonzero distance from the seventh invariant's reflection sign-flip) -- the two numbers telling a
# surface whether handedness matters. A shape against itself reads distance exactly zero and the distance is symmetric in its two
# arguments (the metric laws); the signed-log transform preserves each invariant's sign and folds a sub-floor magnitude to
# exactly zero. No network, no key, no funds -- the "are these the same shape?" a surface asks, on our own open geometry.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/s/shape_match_witness.rish

say "shape_match: Language EN -- Season G shape recognizer over two HuSet fingerprints; shape_distance sums the absolute signed-log differences of the six reflection-invariant Hu moments (a mark and its mirror read the same form), shape_distance_oriented adds the seventh so handedness is seen."
say "shape_match: Style Radiant -- compose not braid: owns only the metric (the signed-log transform and the sum of absolute differences); hu_moments owns the invariants, label the LabelMap. No pixel read, no image written, no codec touched -- a pure function over two seven-number fingerprints."
say "shape_match: Lens TAME -- the signed logs run in f64 exactly as hu_moments reads its invariants so no accumulator can wrap; a sub-floor magnitude folds to exactly zero so a symmetric shape's zero invariants never become a negative infinity; the compared span is bounded by the seven-long signature; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/shape_match"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/shape_match.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "shape_match: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "shape_match: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "shape_match: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-shape-match" else "shape_match: selftest did not print the GREEN line"

say "shape_match: GREEN -- the same triangle turned, mirrored, and scaled reads a tiny distance to the base while a different shape (a plus) reads a large one (the recognizer no single Hu value gives), the reflection-invariant metric reads a mark and its mirror as one form while the oriented metric sees the chirality of the seventh invariant, a shape against itself reads zero, the distance is symmetric, the signed-log transform folds a sub-floor magnitude to zero, on metal."
